FAQ = [
 ("What are the best running boards for a 2021–2026 Ford Bronco?",
  "Rough Country's BA2 is the best running board for a 2021–2026 Bronco 4-door. It is a 5 in steel board with a no-drill bolt-on install and a 5-year limited warranty, and Rough Country lists it for 2021–2026. Rough Country's dual-motor power boards fold away for trail clearance and carry a 660 lb per-side rating. For the 2-door, Westin's Outlaw Drop gives the lowest step, and Rough Country's oval nerf steps are the budget bar if the rocker is bare. Go Rhino's D1 has the longest warranty once the seller confirms the door count. Count the doors and look for factory rock rails first, since most steps here go on in place of them."),
 ("Do 2-door Bronco running boards fit the 4-door?",
  "No. A side step runs between the wheel wells, and the two bodies are different lengths there. Wikipedia lists a 100.4 in wheelbase for the 2-door Bronco and 116.1 in for the 4-door, a gap of 15.7 in, with overall lengths of 174.8 in against 189.4 to 190.5 in. Every maker we read sells the two bodies as separate parts. Rough Country's BA2 board is 41008 for the 2-door and 41007 for the 4-door, and Westin's Outlaw Drop is 20-14185 for the 2-door and 20-14195 for the 4-door. Count your doors, then buy a listing whose title names that count."),
 ("Do Bronco Sport running boards fit the full-size Bronco?",
  "No. The Bronco Sport shares the name and nothing under the doors. Wikipedia describes the Bronco Sport as a compact crossover, while the sixth-generation Bronco is based on the Ford Ranger pickup. Rough Country keeps a separate Bronco Sport page for steps and running boards, and the Amazon title for Westin's 20-14185 says it excludes the Bronco Sport. Search results mix the two vehicles constantly. Skip any listing that says Sport, and be careful with one that says only Bronco next to a photo of a crossover."),
 ("Which Broncos come with factory rock rails or side steps?",
  "Ford's accessory store sells the factory rock rails as a part and says they are the OE rails that come standard on Black Diamond, Badlands and First Edition models. A Bronco6G owner reading Ford's Build and Price tool reported a powder-coated tube step as standard on the Outer Banks; we could not confirm that on a Ford page. We also could not confirm what the Wildtrak, Big Bend, Everglades or Raptor carry, or whether equipment changed in later model years. Two Amazon titles for rail-mounted steps exclude the 2025 Badlands and Big Bend, which may point to a change we could not confirm. Look under your doors and check the window sticker."),
 ("Can I add steps to a Bronco that already has factory rock rails?",
  "There are two routes. The first is to take the rails off. Rough Country says its power running boards need factory rock sliders removed, RealTruck says Go Rhino's Dominator D1 requires removal of the OE rock rails if equipped, and Rough Country says its 2-door oval nerf steps do not fit models with factory rock sliders at all. The second route is an add-on step that bolts to the rail itself. The ROADER listing in our product list names the Bronco with rock rail, 2-door and 4-door, for 2021 to 2024. The first route gives a wider step. The second keeps the armor."),
 ("Do these steps fit the Bronco Raptor?",
  "We could not confirm it for any pick. Wikipedia says the Raptor arrived for 2022 as a 4-door only and is wider than other Broncos at 85.7 in. None of the Rough Country, Westin or RealTruck pages we read names the Raptor as a fit or as an exclusion, and none of the six pick titles names it. One rail-mounted step listing we saw says in its title that it does not fit the Raptor. If you own a Raptor, send the seller your VIN and ask for the part number in writing before you order."),
 ("Do these steps fit a Bronco with the Sasquatch package?",
  "No maker page we read mentions the Sasquatch package, so treat it as unconfirmed. The package's 35 in tires make the sill sit higher off the ground. Westin lists its Outlaw Drop for the Bronco as a rocker panel mount, and RealTruck says Go Rhino's Dominator D1 mounts to the rocker panel. That is a reason to expect the same brackets to line up, not proof. A taller Bronco also makes a drop step more useful. Ask the seller to confirm the part for a Sasquatch build before you buy."),
 ("Are power running boards worth it on a Bronco?",
  "They are the only type that gives a low step at the curb and nothing below the rocker on the trail. Rough Country lists its dual-motor 4-door kit, PSR51330, at $1,399.95 on its own site, with 6 in aluminum boards, a 660 lb capacity per side and a no-drill install. Its HT-Series 4-door kit is listed there at $999.95 on sale from $1,099.95. The dual-motor kit costs more than three times the BA2 board, and its motors live in mud and road salt. Rough Country also says factory rock sliders must be removed. For a Bronco that stays on pavement, a fixed board does the job for much less."),
 ("What is the difference between a running board and a rock slider?",
  "A running board is built for feet. A rock slider is built to carry the vehicle when the rocker comes down on a rock. Ford's accessory page shows the gap: it describes the factory rock rails as heavy-gauge steel with a load-bearing design, made to support up to 1.5 times the vehicle weight. No running board on this page publishes anything close to that. Rough Country's power boards quote 660 lb per side, which is a rating for people. If the Bronco crawls over rocks, keep or buy rails. If it carries passengers on roads, a board or drop step is easier to use."),
 ("Do I need to drill to install Bronco side steps?",
  "Not for the documented picks. Rough Country describes the BA2 boards as a no-drill, direct bolt-on installation, and says the same of its power boards and its 2-door oval nerf steps. Westin lists the Outlaw Drop as a bolt-on installation with no drilling required and includes the hardware and mounting brackets. RealTruck lists Go Rhino's Dominator D1 as a no-drill installation on most applications. We could not read a maker page for Rough Country's Rock Sliders or for the Hooke Road drop steps in our product list, so check those listings for the mounting method before the Bronco goes up on stands."),
 ("How much weight can Bronco running boards hold?",
  "Only one maker page we read gives a number. Rough Country quotes a 660 lb weight capacity per side for its power running boards. Its BA2 and oval nerf pages give no rating, Westin gives none for the Outlaw Drop, and RealTruck gives none for Go Rhino's Dominator D1. One budget listing in our product list, from Broaddict, states 350 lb in its title. A rating covers a person stepping, per side. It does not make the board a jacking point or a tie-down. If the listing gives no figure and a heavy rider will use the step, ask the seller first."),
]

